const faqs = [
  { question: 'How long to smoke beef jerky?', answer: 'Smoke beef jerky at 160–180°F for 4–6 hours for 1/4-inch slices. Start checking at 4 hours using the bend test. In a Traeger or pellet smoker at 165°F, plan for 4–5 hours. In a traditional charcoal smoker, plan 5–7 hours due to less consistent temperature control.' },
  { question: 'What temperature to smoke beef jerky?', answer: 'Smoke beef jerky at 160–180°F (71–82°C). The USDA recommends reaching 160°F internal temperature. Pellet smokers and electric smokers are easiest to hold at this range. Avoid smoking above 200°F — higher temperatures cook the meat rather than dehydrate it, producing a tougher, less traditional jerky texture.' },
  { question: 'Can you make beef jerky in a smoker?', answer: 'Yes — a smoker produces the most flavorful beef jerky possible. The combination of low-temperature drying and smoke absorption is superior to an oven or dehydrator for flavor depth. Electric, pellet, and charcoal smokers all work. Electric and pellet smokers are easiest because they maintain consistent temperatures without babysitting.' },
  { question: 'Dehydrator vs smoker for beef jerky — which is better?', answer: 'Dehydrators are better for convenience, consistency, and food safety. Smokers produce better flavor. For the best of both worlds: smoke the marinated beef at 160°F for 2 hours to absorb smoke flavor, then finish in a dehydrator at 160°F for 2–3 hours for perfectly dried results. This hybrid method is increasingly popular with serious jerky makers.' },
  { question: 'How do I make beef jerky on a Traeger pellet grill?', answer: 'Set your Traeger to 165°F (Smoke setting or the lowest temp available). Place marinated, dried beef strips directly on the grill grates or on a jerky rack. Smoke for 4–5 hours, checking hourly after the 3-hour mark. Use applewood or cherry pellets for a mild, sweet smoke that complements most jerky marinades without overpowering.' },
];

