---
<PillarLayout
  title="Smoked Beef Jerky Recipe — Traeger, Electric & Charcoal"
  description="Smoked beef jerky recipe for Traeger, electric smoker, and charcoal. Temperature, smoke wood guide, and step-by-step instructions for the best smoked jerky."
  {canonical}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Smoked Beef Jerky', href: '/smoked-beef-jerky/' }]}
  {faqs}
  {relatedLinks}
  dateModified="June 2024"
>
  <h1>Smoked Beef Jerky</h1>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p>Smoked beef jerky delivers a depth of flavor that no dehydrator or oven can replicate. Smoke at <strong>160–180°F</strong> for <strong>4–6 hours</strong> using apple, cherry, or hickory wood. A pellet smoker (like a Traeger) makes the process nearly foolproof — set the temperature and walk away.</p>
  </div>

  <img src="/images/smoked-beef-jerky-smoker.webp" alt="Pellet smoker lid open showing beef jerky strips on grates with wisps of smoke" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:1.5rem 0;" loading="lazy" />

  <h2>Smoked Beef Jerky Marinade</h2>
  <div class="recipe-section">
    <ul>
      <li>2 lbs eye of round or top round, sliced 1/4-inch thick</li>
      <li>1/3 cup soy sauce</li>
      <li>2 tablespoons Worcestershire sauce</li>
      <li>1 tablespoon liquid smoke (skip if using a real smoker)</li>
      <li>2 teaspoons black pepper, coarsely ground</li>
      <li>1 teaspoon garlic powder</li>
      <li>1 teaspoon onion powder</li>
      <li>1 teaspoon smoked paprika</li>
      <li>1/2 teaspoon cayenne (optional)</li>
      <li>1 tablespoon brown sugar</li>
      <li>1/4 teaspoon Prague Powder No. 1</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Marinate 12–24 hours, pat dry, then smoke.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>How to Make Beef Jerky in a Smoker — Step by Step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Prep and marinate the beef.</strong> Slice 1/4 inch thick, marinate 12–24 hours, then pat completely dry with paper towels.</li>
    <li><strong>Preheat your smoker.</strong> Target 160–175°F. Add your chosen wood chips, pellets, or chunks.</li>
    <li><strong>Lay strips on grates or jerky racks.</strong> Space strips so air circulates around each piece. For pellet grills, use a jerky rack or the upper grate to keep strips from falling through.</li>
    <li><strong>Smoke at 160–175°F.</strong> Maintain this temperature range throughout. Add more wood/pellets as needed for consistent smoke.</li>
    <li><strong>Check at 3 hours.</strong> Rotate strips from the hotter spots to the cooler edges. Check more frequently after 4 hours.</li>
    <li><strong>Test doneness at 4–5 hours.</strong> Use the bend test: done jerky cracks and bends without snapping. It should feel firm and dry with no glossy surface moisture.</li>
    <li><strong>Cool on a wire rack</strong> for 1 hour before storing.</li>
  </ol>

  <img src="/images/beef-jerky-marinade-bag.webp" alt="Beef jerky strips marinating in soy sauce and spice mixture in zip-lock bag" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:1.5rem 0;" loading="lazy" />

  <h2>Best Wood for Smoking Beef Jerky</h2>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Wood</th><th>Flavor Profile</th><th>Best Pairing</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Applewood</td><td>Mild, sweet, fruity</td><td>Teriyaki, honey, most recipes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cherry</td><td>Sweet, slightly tart, deep color</td><td>Spicy, sweet, classic</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hickory</td><td>Strong, bacon-like</td><td>Classic, BBQ, peppered</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mesquite</td><td>Very strong, earthy</td><td>Bold spice blends — use sparingly</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pecan</td><td>Nutty, mild, rich</td><td>Sweet, garlic, traditional</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Alder</td><td>Light, delicate</td><td>Any recipe; beginner-friendly</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <img src="/images/beef-jerky-done.webp" alt="Finished smoked beef jerky strips in dark mahogany color with smoky crust on slate" style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:1.5rem 0;" loading="lazy" />

  <h2>Tillamook Country Smoker Beef Jerky Copycat</h2>
  <p>Tillamook Country Smoker is one of the most popular commercial smoked beef jerky brands. To replicate their signature "Old Fashioned" flavor at home: use a heavier hand of black pepper (1.5 tsp), skip sweeteners entirely, smoke with hickory for 5 hours at 165°F, and add 1/2 teaspoon of liquid smoke to the marinade for extra depth. The result is a dry, peppery, intensely smoky jerky.</p>

  <h2>Traeger Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>Traeger pellet grills are excellent for beef jerky because the auger system maintains steady temperature without manual fuel management. Best settings:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Set to <strong>165°F</strong> (or the Smoke setting if your model has it)</li>
    <li>Use <strong>applewood or cherry pellets</strong> for mild, food-friendly smoke</li>
    <li>Total time: <strong>4–5 hours</strong> at 165°F</li>
    <li>Place a sheet of aluminum foil on the lowest grate to catch drips</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Electric Smoker Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>Electric smokers like the Masterbuilt hold temperature precisely, making them ideal for jerky. Use wood chips in the tray, set to 160°F, and plan for 4–6 hours. Rotate the racks from bottom to top midway through, as electric smokers often run hotter at the bottom.</p>

